\section{The team punting block}{


The two team endpoints carry one 24-column block twice: \verb{punting_*} covers the
team's own punts (so \code{punting_return_yards} is what opponents returned against
it) and \verb{returning_*} covers the punts it received (so \code{returning_punts} counts
opponent punts and \code{returning_return_yards} is the team's own return yardage).
In a game frame, one side's \verb{punting_*} row matches its opponent's
\verb{returning_*} row.

The block is the first 15 columns of the player punting block above, then:\tabular{lll}{
   col_name \tab type \tab description \cr
   returns \tab integer \tab Punt returns. \cr
   return_yards_available \tab integer \tab Returns with known yards (the denominator for \code{yards_per_return}). \cr
   return_yards \tab integer \tab Return yards over those returns. \cr
   yards_per_return \tab double \tab Yards per return. \cr
   longest_return \tab integer \tab Longest return, in yards. \cr
   returns20plus \tab integer \tab Returns of 20 or more yards. \cr
   return_touchdowns \tab integer \tab Returns for a touchdown. \cr
   muffs \tab integer \tab Muffed punts. \cr
   receiving_team_turnovers \tab integer \tab Punts on which the receiving team lost possession, such as on a muff or fumble. \cr
}


The \verb{*_available} counts are denominators, not statistics. CFBD parses these
fields out of play text, so a punt can be counted while its yardage stays
unknown. Dividing by \code{punts} or \code{returns} instead of the matching
\verb{*_available} column understates every mean.
}

\section{Season coverage}{


Unlike the passing and rushing families, these endpoints reach back to
\strong{2015}. Earlier seasons answer HTTP 200 with an empty array rather than an
error, so a request for 2014 returns an empty data frame, not a failure.
}

